{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And the sons of Jacob answered Shechem and Hamor his father with deception.</b> It would have been proper for Jacob to speak, for they were before him; but because the matter was a disgrace to them, the sons did not want the father to speak of it or open his mouth in this at all, for they would speak in his place — and they did this for the honor of the father." "<b>And the Ramban wrote</b> that since they answered with the consent and counsel of their father, and he knew that they spoke with deception, if so why was he angry with Simeon and Levi, saying to them ״עכרתם אותי״ — 'you have troubled me'? And furthermore, it is not fitting to give his daughter to a Canaanite who had defiled her. Now all the brothers made this entire answer with deception, and Simeon and Levi alone performed the deed, and their father cursed their anger — them alone. And the answer is that the deception was in their saying to circumcise every male, for they thought that the men of the city would not do so; and if perhaps they would heed their prince and all be circumcised, they would come on the third day when they were in pain and take their sister from the house of Shechem — and this was the counsel of all the brothers with their father's permission. But Simeon and Levi wanted to take vengeance upon them and killed the entire city; therefore he cursed their anger, because they killed the men of the city who had not sinned against them. And the permissibility that the sons of Jacob — Simeon and Levi — found to shed innocent blood, the Rambam, of blessed memory, wrote: because the sons of Noah were commanded concerning seven commandments, and the laws of justice are included among them; and a son of Noah who transgressed and stole or robbed or forced his fellow's daughter or seduced her is liable to the death penalty by the sword; and likewise anyone who saw another transgress one of them and did not judge him to be killed — that witness is himself killed by the sword. And because of this, all the men of Shechem were liable, for Shechem had robbed and forced, and they saw him and knew and did not judge him. But the Ramban wrote concerning this that it is not possible to say so, for the matter was not given over to Jacob and his sons to execute judgment upon them. Rather, the permissibility that Simeon and Levi found is explained by the fact that the men of Shechem were wicked, idolaters, and given to sexual immorality, as it is written of them (Leviticus 18:27): ״כי את כל התועבות האל עשו אנשי הארץ״ — 'for all these abominations have the men of the land done,' etc. Therefore they considered their blood as insignificant as water and wanted to take vengeance upon them, and they killed the prince and all the men of the city, for all of them followed his way — end quote."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:40.741871266Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}